provider_responses.text:
A few points could easily be overlooked or misunderstood:

### 1. Six months may not mean only six months of contributions

Eligibility is not always based only on the current job. Earlier registered work can count too, if it falls within the relevant look-back period.

For example, someone who worked six months for the current employer but had another registered job before that may have enough total contribution days to qualify for support.

So the right question is not only:

> “How long have I worked at this job?”

It is:

> “How many registered contribution days do I have across all qualifying work during the relevant period?”

### 2. “Fixed-term contract ending” is not automatically problem-free

An employer may choose not to renew a fixed-term contract, but there can still be rules about:

- notice;
- the reason for using a fixed-term contract;
- repeated renewals;
- final compensation;
- whether the contract should legally have been treated as open-ended.

So a worker should not assume that “my contract ended” means there is nothing to check.

### 3. Probation periods are not unlimited

An employer cannot simply call someone “on probation” forever. The contract should state the probation arrangement, and the legal duration can depend on the role and contract.

If six months have passed, the probation period may already be over in many ordinary situations. It is worth checking the contract rather than accepting the employer’s description.

### 4. Signing does not always mean the worker agrees—but it can create problems

Sometimes workers are asked to sign documents merely to confirm receipt. But a document may also contain wording such as:

- “I resign voluntarily”;
- “I accept the termination by mutual agreement”;
- “I confirm all amounts have been paid”;
- “I have no further claims.”

Signing such wording can make a later dispute more difficult. If the worker signs only to acknowledge receipt, they can write something like:

> Received on [date], without accepting the contents or waiving any rights.

They should keep a copy or photograph of the document.

### 5. Final payments may arrive after the last working day

The worker should check not only whether money was received immediately, but whether the final payroll calculation is correct once it arrives. A small payment can look plausible while leaving out holiday pay, overtime, supplements, or proportional annual payments.

Compare the settlement with past payslips, schedules, and the contract.

### 6. Informal work creates an extra problem

If the employer did not formally register the worker, did not issue payslips, or paid cash without records, proving contributions and wages becomes harder. However, that does not mean the worker has no claim.

Useful evidence can include:

- bank transfers;
- work schedules;
- messages with managers;
- photographs in uniform or at the workplace;
- coworker statements;
- location records;
- emails;
- records of shifts worked.

### 7. Deadlines matter

A worker may have limited time to register for support or challenge a dismissal. Waiting because they are unsure what happened can make the situation worse.

The safest approach is to register promptly, preserve documents, and seek advice early—even if the worker is still deciding whether to challenge the employer.

